You can think about your code as data being carried from one API to another:



LOGIN
  │
  │ response
  ↓
TOKEN
  │
  │ Authorization: Bearer token
  ↓
CREATE EVENT
  │
  │ response
  ↓
EVENT ID
  │
  ├──────────────→ GET EVENTS
  │                    │
  │                    ↓
  │              Verify event exists
  │
  ↓
DELETE EVENT
  │
  │ DELETE /events/{eventId}
  ↓
GET EVENTS AGAIN
  │
  ↓
Verify event does NOT exist




In one sentence:
Login gives me the token, Create Event uses the token and gives me the eventId, GET verifies that eventId exists,
DELETE uses the same token + eventId, and the final GET verifies that the eventId no longer exists.

That is your complete  [ Login → Create → GET → Delete → Verify Delete ]  API chaining flow.
This is a good example of API chaining.